 "name": "Ornamental Plants in Urban Green Space: Preference (rating)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Zheng, J., Huang, Y., Chen, Y., Guan, L., & Liu, Q. (2022). Subjective preference and visual attention to the attributes of ornamental plants in urban green space: an eye-tracking study. Forests 13:1871. The 19 photographs of the paper's Supplementary Figure S1 show four attributes of ornamental plants: vegetation type (I-A grassland, I-B shrub, I-C broad-leaf tree, I-D coniferous tree, I-E bamboo, I-F palm), flower colour (II-A blue, II-B red, II-C pink, II-D purple, II-E white, II-F yellow), leaf colour (III-A green, III-B yellow, III-C red) and layer (IV-A tree + shrub + grass, IV-B shrub + grass, IV-C shrub, IV-D grass). As in the paper, participants first view every photograph for 15 seconds with a 5-second blank between them, then rate their preference for each on a five-point scale (1 strongly dislike, 2 dislike, 3 average, 4 like, 5 strongly like). Gender, age and home climate zone are asked at the end. Analysis as in the paper: one-way ANOVA with Tukey HSD within each attribute category. Original result (90 participants aged 20–35, 90% from tropical or subtropical regions): tree + shrub + grass, blue, red and pink flowers, broad-leaf trees and bamboo were preferred; coniferous trees, shrubs, white flowers, green leaves and grassland scored lowest, but differences within categories were not significant.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the original recorded eye movements (EyeLink 1000 Plus) during viewing; no eye tracking here. (2) Viewing and rating orders are random for each participant; the paper does not state its order. (3) In the paper the ratings were collected on a questionnaire after the viewing; here each photograph is shown again while it is rated. (4) The home-climate question and the \"Other / prefer not to say\" option are ours; the paper only reports this split for its sample.\n\nPhotographs: Supplementary Figure S1 of the paper (CC BY 4.0), used as published with credit to the authors; the template is removed if the authors object.",
